On the first day of the 2015 IECS conference in 2014, we made a joint presentation on the power of learning to support the transition to sustainable technologies.
The aim was to raise awareness about how online platforms can enable social inclusion and environmental sustainability for communities living in remote areas and those who are not well-connected. Our topic was “Learning from Challenges” – which is an initiative by the European Commission, through its ICT and Sustainable Development Programme (ESSDP) to increase the visibility of e-learning in the field of inclusive education. This project aimed at creating an understanding between digital and human development in Europe.
We gathered several interesting facts regarding the challenges that were faced by young people as they transitioned into digital environments. The most common ones were:
• low participation rates;
• lack of access to high quality ICT resources and devices;
• lack of flexibility;
• lack of training;
• inability to transfer skills learned in school or at home;
• lack of proper access to ICT equipment;
• poor internet connectivity;
• lack of adequate technology infrastructure.
These issues have been identified by all stakeholders and highlighted by many different organisations and individuals around the world.
